-- §14.5.3: derived `Hashable` MUST be structurally aligned with the
-- type's `Eq` semantics, and §14.5.1 makes derive a required portable
-- path checked by generated-proof typechecking — a derivation is never
-- accepted (or rejected) merely because the implementation says so. Q
-- carries only an Equiv instance (a coarse equivalence, not Eq), so
-- `derive Hashable Q` has no Eq semantics to align with and must fail
-- with exactly one error. NOTE: today the single error is E_UNSUPPORTED
-- (derive declarations unimplemented) rather than the real target — the
-- missing `Eq Q` / Hashable-refines-Eq rejection of §14.5.3. The count
-- assertion holds across both, but the blanket-E_UNSUPPORTED rationale is
-- no longer spec-sanctioned (§14.5.1); re-point the assertion to the
-- Eq-alignment diagnostic when the derivation path lands.
--! mode check
--! assertErrorCount 1
module main

import std.hash.*

data Q : Type =
    MkQ (v : Int)

instance Equiv Q =
    let (~=) a b = True

derive Hashable Q

-- STATUS(a51e1bf): PASS — but only because 'derive' is wholly unsupported (E_UNSUPPORTED); re-point to the Equiv-specific rejection when a derivation path lands.
